"""English — details, sira_c_1 (50 questions)."""

D = {
"sira_151": "Every standard biography records that Hamza and the Prophet ﷺ were nursed by the same woman, so the uncle was also a foster brother close in age. Hamza declared his Islam after hearing that Abu Jahl had insulted his nephew at the Ka'bah; from that day Quraysh knew the Prophet ﷺ had a protector who would not bend. He fought at Badr and was killed at Uhud, where the Prophet ﷺ stood over his body in grief. A Muslim remembers Hamza as courage placed at the service of faith.",
"sira_152": "The sira records that al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam, one of the earliest converts, opened his house to the Prophet ﷺ when open teaching in Makkah was impossible. There the Prophet ﷺ read the revelation to the small band of believers and prayed with them out of sight of Quraysh. It was to this house that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab came to declare his Islam, after which the Muslims went out to pray at the Ka'bah openly. A Muslim learns that the community grew from patient teaching in a single room.",
"sira_153": "Every standard biography records that Mus'ab ibn 'Umayr grew up in comfort and left it all when he accepted Islam, his family cutting him off. In Yathrib he taught the Qur'an house by house and was called the reader, al-muqri'; through him Islam entered nearly every household of the city before the Hijra. At Uhud he carried the banner and was killed, and his shroud was too short to cover both his head and his feet. A Muslim learns that one patient teacher can prepare a whole city.",
"sira_154": "The surah continues: \"O you who covers himself, arise and warn, and your Lord glorify, and your clothing purify, and uncleanliness avoid\" (74:1-5). The one addressed is the Prophet ﷺ, who had wrapped himself in a garment after the first encounter with the angel; the Qur'an now calls him out of that covering to a task. The first revelation, \"Read\" (96:1), made him a prophet; these verses made him a messenger to others. A Muslim learns that receiving the truth is followed by carrying it to people.",
"sira_155": "The verse and the one after it read: \"So declare openly what you are commanded and turn away from the polytheists. Indeed, We are sufficient for you against the mockers\" (15:94-95). It belongs to Surah al-Hijr, revealed in Makkah while the call was still quiet, and the promise that follows answers the fear of what open preaching would bring. Another verse told him to begin with his nearest relatives (26:214). A Muslim learns that speaking the truth openly comes with a promise of Allah's protection.",
"sira_156": "The sira records that Quraysh swore neither to marry into nor to trade with Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib until they surrendered the Prophet ﷺ, and the whole clan withdrew with Abu Talib into their valley, where hunger pressed them for years. The Prophet ﷺ then told Abu Talib that termites had eaten the document except the name of Allah written in it; when the sheet was taken down, it was found as he had said. A Muslim learns that Allah brings an unjust pact to nothing.",
"sira_157": "Every standard biography records that 'Addas, a Christian from Nineveh, heard the Prophet ﷺ say \"In the name of Allah\" before eating, words he had never heard from the people of that land. When the Prophet ﷺ learned his city, he said 'Addas came from the town of the righteous man Yunus, son of Matta, his brother in prophethood; 'Addas kissed his head and hands. On the road back the Prophet ﷺ was consoled that his message was heard. A Muslim learns that kindness reaches a stranger before argument does.",
"sira_158": "The sira records that 'Umar set out sword in hand intending to kill the Prophet ﷺ, and on the way was told that his own sister Fatima and her husband Sa'id ibn Zayd had become Muslims. He went to their house, heard the beginning of Surah Ta-Ha, and asked to be taken to the Prophet ﷺ, then in the house of al-Arqam. After his Islam the Muslims prayed at the Ka'bah openly. A Muslim learns that a hard heart can turn in a single hour of listening to the Qur'an.",
"sira_159": "Every standard biography records that as the Prophet ﷺ entered Madinah, the Ansar begged him to stay with them, and he said to let the camel go, for she was commanded. She knelt on land belonging to two orphan boys, where the mosque was afterwards built, and the nearest house was that of Abu Ayyub, who carried the Prophet's ﷺ saddle inside. A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ let Allah choose his host, not rank.",
"sira_160": "The name Baqi' al-Gharqad comes from the thorn trees, gharqad, that once grew on the ground beside the Prophet's ﷺ mosque. The Prophet ﷺ used to go out to al-Baqi' at night, greet its people as believers, and pray for their forgiveness, saying the living would soon join them. His infant son Ibrahim, several of his wives, and companions of every rank were laid there. A Muslim visiting Madinah greets the people of al-Baqi' in the words the Prophet ﷺ used and remembers that death gathers everyone.",
"sira_161": "The verse reads: \"A mosque founded on righteousness from the first day is more worthy for you to stand in. Within it are men who love to purify themselves, and Allah loves those who purify themselves\" (9:108). It follows the condemnation of a mosque the hypocrites built for harm (9:107), which the Prophet ﷺ is forbidden to stand in. Quba is where he stopped and laid a foundation on arriving at Madinah. A Muslim learns that a place of prayer is honoured by the intention it was built with.",
"sira_162": "Every standard biography records that the Muslims had prayed facing Jerusalem while the Prophet ﷺ longed to face the Ka'bah. The Qur'an answered: \"We have certainly seen the turning of your face toward the heaven, and We will surely turn you to a qiblah with which you will be pleased. So turn your face toward al-Masjid al-Haram\" (2:144). The prayer in that mosque began facing one direction and ended facing the other. A Muslim learns that a command is obeyed the moment it is heard.",
"sira_163": "The Qur'an gives the command: \"O you who have believed, decreed upon you is fasting as it was decreed upon those before you, that you may become righteous\" (2:183), and names the month: \"The month of Ramadan in which was revealed the Qur'an\" (2:185). Every standard biography records that this first Ramadan of fasting was also the month in which the battle of Badr was fought. A Muslim learns that Ramadan was given as a month of self-restraint and of the Qur'an together.",
"sira_164": "The sira records that the Muslims, lacking a way to gather for prayer, discussed a bell or a horn, and neither pleased the Prophet ﷺ. 'Abdullah ibn Zayd then saw in a dream a man teaching him the words of the call; the Prophet ﷺ declared it a true dream and had him teach it to Bilal, whose voice carried further. 'Umar came and said he had seen the same. A Muslim learns that the adhan heard five times a day began with a dream confirmed by the Prophet ﷺ.",
"sira_165": "The verse reads in full: \"And you did not kill them, but it was Allah who killed them. And you threw not when you threw, but it was Allah who threw, that He might test the believers with a good test from Him\" (8:17). Surah al-Anfal was revealed about Badr, and the verse addresses the Prophet ﷺ after he cast a handful of dust toward the enemy lines. A Muslim learns to work with effort and to credit victory to Allah alone.",
"sira_166": "The passage reads: \"As your Lord brought you out of your home in truth, while a party of the believers were unwilling, arguing with you concerning the truth after it had become clear, as if they were being driven toward death while they were looking on\" (8:5-6). The next verse gives the cause: they had hoped for the unarmed caravan, and Allah wished to establish the truth instead (8:7). A Muslim learns that reluctance before a hard duty is human, and obedience through it is faith.",
"sira_167": "The verse reads: \"And remember when you were few and oppressed in the land, fearing that people might abduct you, but He sheltered you, supported you with His victory, and provided you with good things, that you might be grateful\" (8:26). It stands in Surah al-Anfal before the warning not to betray Allah and His Messenger (8:27). The sheltering is the welcome in Madinah, the victory is Badr. A Muslim learns to look back at times of weakness so that today's strength is met with thanks.",
"sira_168": "Every standard biography records al-Hubab's exact question: was this a position Allah had ordered, or a matter of opinion, war and stratagem? Told it was the latter, he proposed to advance to the well nearest the enemy, fill in the other wells, and build a cistern so the Muslims would drink and their enemy would not; the Prophet ﷺ said he had spoken well and moved at once. A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ welcomed sound counsel in matters not settled by revelation.",
"sira_169": "The Qur'an names the place itself: \"And already had Allah given you victory at Badr while you were few in number\" (3:123). Surah al-Anfal describes the ground: \"when you were on the near side of the valley, and they were on the farther side, and the caravan was lower than you\" (8:42). The caravan under Abu Sufyan had escaped by the coast, so the Muslims met the army of Makkah instead. A Muslim learns that Badr, the first great test, is remembered in the Qur'an by its own name.",
"sira_170": "The passage reads: \"It is not for a prophet to have captives until he inflicts a massacre in the land. You desire the commodities of this world, but Allah desires the Hereafter\" (8:67), then: \"So consume what you have taken of war booty as being lawful and good\" (8:69). The next verse tells the prisoners that if Allah knows any good in their hearts He will give them better than what was taken (8:70). A Muslim learns that Allah's correction came together with His forgiveness.",
"sira_171": "The verse reads: \"And Allah had certainly fulfilled His promise to you when you were killing them by His permission until, when you lost courage and fell to disputing about the order and disobeyed after He had shown you that which you love\" (3:152). It continues that some among them desired this world and some the Hereafter, and that Allah had forgiven them. The following verse recalls their flight while the Messenger ﷺ called them from behind (3:153). A Muslim learns that the promise of help stands while obedience stands.",
"sira_172": "The verse continues: \"rejoicing in what Allah has bestowed upon them of His bounty, and they receive good tidings about those after them who have not yet joined them, that there will be no fear concerning them, nor will they grieve\" (3:169-170). It belongs to the part of Surah Al-'Imran revealed after Uhud, when the survivors mourned their dead. The Qur'an answers their grief with a picture of life, provision and joy with their Lord. A Muslim learns that death in Allah's cause is a passage into His keeping.",
"sira_173": "Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ put the archers under 'Abdullah ibn Jubayr and told them not to move whether the battle went for them or against them. When the Makkans first fled, most of the archers left the hill to gather spoils, and the cavalry of Quraysh came round it and struck the Muslims from behind. The Qur'an refers to this as disputing about the order and disobeying (3:152). A Muslim learns that a post held is worth more than a prize taken.",
"sira_174": "The verse continues: \"There the believers were tested and shaken with a severe shaking\" (33:10-11). The two directions are the confederate armies outside the city and the treachery within it. The Qur'an then contrasts two voices: the hypocrites said that Allah and His Messenger had promised them only delusion (33:12), while the believers, seeing the armies, said this was what Allah and His Messenger had promised them (33:22). A Muslim learns that fear itself is no sin; what matters is what the heart says under it.",
"sira_175": "The verse reads in full: \"And Allah repelled those who disbelieved, in their rage, not having obtained any good. And sufficient was Allah for the believers in battle, and ever is Allah Powerful and Exalted in Might\" (33:25). Earlier in the surah the means are named: \"when armies came to you and We sent upon them a wind and armies you did not see\" (33:9). The confederates left without a pitched battle. A Muslim learns that Allah can end a threat without a sword being raised.",
"sira_176": "Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ set out meaning only to perform 'umrah, and that his camel al-Qaswa knelt at Hudaybiyya and would not go on. There, under a tree, the companions gave him the pledge the Qur'an praises: \"Certainly was Allah pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you under the tree\" (48:18). The treaty sent them home that year with a promise to return. A Muslim learns that a halt on the edge of the Haram became a door to the conquest.",
"sira_177": "The verse continues: \"with your heads shaved and hair shortened, not fearing. He knew what you did not know and has arranged before that a conquest near at hand\" (48:27). When the treaty turned the company back the vision seemed unfulfilled; the Qur'an declares it true and only delayed. The same surah opens by calling the treaty itself a clear conquest (48:1). A Muslim learns that a promise of Allah is fulfilled in His time, and a delay is not a denial.",
"sira_178": "The verse reads in full: \"And it is He who withheld their hands from you and your hands from them within the area of Makkah after He caused you to overcome them, and Allah is Seeing of what you do\" (48:24). The next verse explains the restraint: believing men and women unknown to the Muslims were in Makkah and might have been trampled had fighting begun (48:25). A Muslim learns that a battle avoided at Hudaybiyya was a mercy Allah counts among His favours.",
"sira_179": "Every standard biography records that Khaybar followed the truce with Quraysh, and the Qur'an promises those who pledged at Hudaybiyya \"much booty which you will take\" (48:20). The Prophet ﷺ gave the banner to 'Ali ibn Abi Talib on the day the strongest fort fell, and the people of Khaybar were left on their land to work it for a share of the harvest. A Muslim learns that the seventh year turned a treaty many had grieved over into an opening.",
"sira_180": "The sira records that the Muslims entered Makkah for three days as the treaty allowed, while Quraysh withdrew to the surrounding hills to watch. The Prophet ﷺ told his companions to walk briskly in the first rounds of the tawaf so that Quraysh, who said the fever of Madinah had weakened them, would see their strength. This 'umrah completed the one from which they had been turned back the year before. A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ kept the treaty to the letter, even when its terms were hard.",
"sira_181": "Every standard biography records that after Hudaybiyya the Prophet ﷺ sent letters calling the rulers of the surrounding lands to Islam: the Byzantine emperor, the Negus of Abyssinia, the ruler of Egypt and the king of Persia. Chosroes tore the letter to pieces on reading it, and the Prophet ﷺ, when told, asked Allah to tear his kingdom likewise. The same letters were received with respect by others. A Muslim learns that the message was offered to every ruler alike, and that each answered for himself.",
"sira_182": "The sira records that the Negus had already sheltered the first emigrants years earlier and refused to hand them over to the envoys of Quraysh. 'Amr ibn Umayya ad-Damri carried the Prophet's ﷺ letter, and the Negus received it with honour and sent the remaining emigrants, Ja'far ibn Abi Talib among them, back by ship; they reached the Prophet ﷺ at Khaybar. When the Negus died, the Prophet ﷺ led the funeral prayer for him in Madinah. A Muslim learns that a just ruler is remembered and prayed for.",
"sira_183": "Every standard biography records that Khaybar was a cluster of fortresses among palm groves, and that the Prophet ﷺ reached it by night and waited until morning before advancing, since he did not attack a town in which the call to prayer was heard. The forts fell one after another, and the people of Khaybar asked to stay on their land, working it in return for half of what it produced, which was granted. A Muslim learns that the campaign to the north ended in an agreement, not in expulsion.",
"sira_184": "The sira records that Abu Sufyan, the leader of Quraysh, came to the Muslim camp outside Makkah with al-'Abbas, saw the size of the army, and accepted Islam that night. Al-'Abbas said that Abu Sufyan was a man who loved honour and asked the Prophet ﷺ to grant him something; the Prophet ﷺ then made his house a sanctuary alongside the Sacred Mosque and every man's own home. A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ won Makkah by sparing it.",
"sira_185": "Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah in Ramadan with his head bowed low on his camel out of humility before Allah. He went round the Ka'bah and struck down the idols around it, reciting: \"Truth has come, and falsehood has departed. Indeed is falsehood ever bound to depart\" (17:81). Then he asked Quraysh what they expected of him, and told them to go, for they were free. A Muslim learns that the year of the conquest is remembered for forgiveness more than for force.",
"sira_186": "The Qur'an speaks of this battle by name: \"and on the day of Hunayn, when your great number pleased you, but it did not avail you at all, and the earth was confining for you with its vastness; then you turned back, fleeing\" (9:25). The sira records that Hawazin lay in ambush in the valley, and that the Prophet ﷺ held his ground on his mule, calling out that he was the Prophet, until his companions rallied. A Muslim learns that numbers win nothing; steadfastness does.",
"sira_187": "The verse reads in full: \"Then Allah sent down His tranquillity upon His Messenger and upon the believers and sent down soldiers you did not see, and He punished those who disbelieved. And that is the recompense of the disbelievers\" (9:26). The verse before it recalls the flight itself, when the Muslims' great number pleased them and the earth grew narrow (9:25). A Muslim learns that calm sent by Allah, not the size of an army, turns a rout into a victory.",
"sira_188": "The sira records that Thaqif, the tribe of Ta'if, had fought beside Hawazin at Hunayn and, after the defeat, shut themselves inside the walls with provisions for a long stay. The Muslims besieged it for a time, and the Prophet ﷺ lifted the siege, praying that Allah guide Thaqif and bring them to him. Within the year a delegation from Ta'if came to Madinah and accepted Islam. A Muslim learns that the town which had stoned the Prophet ﷺ was won at last by a prayer.",
"sira_189": "The verse reads: \"Allah has already forgiven the Prophet and the Muhajirun and the Ansar who followed him in the hour of difficulty after the hearts of a party of them had almost inclined to doubt, and then He forgave them\" (9:117). The Qur'an explains the difficulty elsewhere: the journey was long (9:42), and those who stayed behind said not to go out in the heat (9:81). A Muslim learns that setting out when the way is hard earns Allah's mercy.",
"sira_190": "The verse reads in full: \"And the three who were left behind, until the earth closed in on them in spite of its vastness and their souls confined them and they were certain that there is no refuge from Allah except in Him. Then He turned to them so they could repent\" (9:118). These three had confessed plainly that they had no excuse. The next verse commands: be with those who are true (9:119), and a Muslim learns that honest confession is the road to forgiveness.",
"sira_191": "The Qur'an records that the expedition was made in fierce heat: those who stayed behind said not to go forth in the heat, and Allah replied that the fire of Hell is more intense in heat (9:81). The Prophet ﷺ marched to Tabuk, found no army waiting, and returned without fighting. On the way back, the Qur'an exposed the mosque built by hypocrites for harm (9:107). A Muslim learns that Tabuk was a test of who would answer the call when the journey was long.",
"sira_192": "Every standard biography records that when the Prophet ﷺ called for help to equip the expedition in a year of drought and heat, 'Uthman brought camels, mounts and money in a quantity no one else matched. The Prophet ﷺ turned the coins over in his hand and said that nothing 'Uthman did after that day would harm him. Abu Bakr brought all he owned and 'Umar half of his. A Muslim learns that giving for the community is measured by what it costs the giver.",
"sira_193": "The sira records that Sa'd's mother swore to neither eat nor drink until he left the religion; the Qur'an answered her: \"But if they endeavour to make you associate with Me that of which you have no knowledge, do not obey them but accompany them in this world with appropriate kindness\" (31:15). At Uhud the Prophet ﷺ urged him to shoot, saying his father and mother were ransom for him. A Muslim learns that firmness in faith and kindness to parents go together.",
"sira_194": "Every standard biography records that az-Zubayr was the son of Safiyya bint 'Abd al-Muttalib, the Prophet's ﷺ aunt, and that he accepted Islam very young in Makkah. During the siege of Madinah, when the Prophet ﷺ asked who would bring him news of the enemy, az-Zubayr volunteered three times, and the Prophet ﷺ said that every prophet has a hawari and his was az-Zubayr. A Muslim learns that the title was earned by stepping forward when others held back.",
"sira_195": "The sira records that on the return from Tabuk a band of hypocrites tried to push the Prophet ﷺ from a mountain pass in the dark, and that Hudhayfa, who was driving his camel, learned who they were. The Prophet ﷺ told him their names and told him to keep them; afterwards 'Umar himself asked Hudhayfa whether his own name was among them. A Muslim learns that a trust kept in silence is a form of loyalty.",
"sira_196": "Every standard biography records that Salman was born in Persia to a father devoted to fire-worship, and left home to seek the Christians' teaching in Syria. The last monk he served told him a prophet would soon appear in a land of palm trees, who would refuse charity, accept gifts and bear the seal of prophethood between his shoulders. Sold as a slave, he reached Madinah and found the three signs in the Prophet ﷺ. A Muslim learns that a sincere seeker is guided across any distance.",
"sira_197": "The sira records that Anas served the Prophet ﷺ for ten years without once being asked why he had done something or why he had not. Umm Sulaym asked the Prophet ﷺ to pray for her little servant, and he asked Allah to increase Anas's wealth and children and to bless him in what He gave, which Anas saw answered. A Muslim learns that a mother's gift of her son to the Prophet's ﷺ service was a gift to the whole community.",
"sira_198": "Every standard biography records that 'Aisha came to the Prophet's ﷺ house in Madinah as a young girl and grew up beside him, and remembered his practice at home more closely than anyone. When the hypocrites slandered her, the Qur'an itself declared her innocence: \"Indeed, those who came with falsehood are a group among you\" (24:11). After the Prophet's ﷺ death in her room, the elder companions brought her their questions. A Muslim learns that much of the Prophet's ﷺ daily life reaches us through her.",
"sira_199": "The verse says: \"So when Zayd had no longer any need for her, We married her to you in order that there not be upon the believers any discomfort concerning the wives of their adopted sons\" (33:37). Zaynab, daughter of the Prophet's ﷺ aunt Umayma, had first been married to Zayd ibn Haritha, his former adopted son; Zayd is the only companion named in the Qur'an. Zaynab used to say that Allah Himself had given her in marriage. A Muslim learns that an adopted son is not a son by blood.",
"sira_200": "The sira records that when the treaty was signed, the companions were so grieved at turning back without 'umrah that the Prophet ﷺ told them three times to rise, sacrifice and shave, and none moved. Umm Salama, who had travelled with him, said to go out, say nothing to anyone, sacrifice his animal and call his barber; he did so, and the companions rose and did the same. A Muslim learns that example moves people where words cannot.",
}
